s a **null pointer** for the returned stack address, so `printLine`'s `if (line != NULL)` check fails and the bad path prints **nothing at all** — not garbage. My `helper_bad()` therefore returns `None`, reproducing that exactly, with the local array retained as a comment-documented no-op.\n\n## Faithful `scanf(\"%d\")` emulation\n\nRather than using `read_line` + `parse` (which would diverge), I hand-rolled the conversion to match C:\n- skips leading whitespace **across newlines** (space, tab, `\\n`, `\\r`, `\\v`, `\\f`)\n- optional `+`/`-`, then requires at least one digit; on matching failure `x` is left **unmodified** at `0` (so bad path)\n- reads one byte at a time so no extra input is consumed\n- overflow follows glibc: clamps to `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN` then truncates to `int`\n\nThat last point was a real bug I caught in differential testing: I initially negated a saturated `LONG_MAX`, so `-99999999999999999999` yielded `1` (→ good path) while C yielded `0` (→ bad path). glibc clamps negative overflow to `LONG_MIN`, which truncates to `0`. Fixed and re-verified.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `cargo build --release` — clean, 0 warnings\n- 32 hand-picked edge cases (empty input, `-`, `+-1`, `0x10`, `.5`, `INT_MIN`/`INT_MAX`, `2^31`, `2^32` wrap-to-zero, whitespace-only, no trailing newline, trailing junk) — all match\n- 3,025-case randomized fuzz over a digit/sign/whitespace/junk alphabet, comparing **stdout bytes and exit code** — 0 mismatches\n- `c_src/` unmodified (verified by mtime/checksum); all files written only under `translation/`\n\nOne note on scope: `4294967296` and `-0` both take the bad path in C, which is genuine C truncation/sign behavior, not an artifact — my implementation reproduces it rather than returning the mathematically \"expected\" nonzero.","ttft_ms":3222,"ttft_stream_ms":3013,"time_to_request_ms":50,"type":"result","duration_ms":135826,"uuid":"d5780d88-19a1-4fd1-a46b-0ad39e688e0b","queued_turn_count":0}
